  "tap for the real-lab rules": "",
  "lab wall reference": "",
  "The simulation can show hazardous chemistry safely, but the same actions in a real laboratory require supervision, protective equipment, and a risk assessment.": "",
  "Wear splash goggles whenever materials are on the bench.": "",
  "heat and flame": "",
  "Keep flammables away from heat. Treat hot glass as hot until measured.": "",
  "fumes and gases": "",
  "Do not smell directly. Use wafting only when the lab explicitly offers it.": "",
  "unexpected result": "",
  "Stop the operation, leave the vessel where it is, and read the warning before continuing.": "",
  "simulation boundary": "",
  "Kerotakis supports learning and planning. It does not replace real laboratory instruction or safety training.": "",
  "edit note": "",
  "delete note": "",
  "mixing bleach with ammonia makes chloramine, a toxic gas": "",
  "People are hospitalised every year from mixing these two household cleaners.": "",
  "mixing bleach with acid releases chlorine, a toxic gas": "",
  "Chlorine gas was used as a chemical weapon; even small amounts damage lungs.": "",
  "a strong oxidizer mixed with a flammable liquid can ignite or explode": "",
  "Potassium permanganate and glycerol ignite on contact; similar mixtures cause laboratory fires.": "",
  "a strong oxidizer in the presence of a flammable gas creates an explosion risk": "",
  "Hydrogen and chlorine mixtures can detonate when exposed to light or a spark.": "",
  "mixing a strong oxidizer with a reducing agent can cause a violent, potentially explosive reaction": "",
  "Permanganate and sulfite solutions react vigorously; at scale, such mixtures can detonate.": "",
  "strong acid dissolves this metal, releasing hydrogen gas which is flammable": "",
  "Magnesium ribbon in hydrochloric acid produces enough hydrogen to pop with a lit splint — a familiar school demo, but the gas is genuinely flammable.": "",
  "strong acid and carbonate fizz vigorously, releasing carbon dioxide — the mixture can spatter": "",
  "Adding acid to chalk or baking soda foams over if the vessel is too small. CO₂ displaces air in enclosed spaces.": "",
  "this water-reactive substance already reacted with water; combining with a strong base adds further heat": "",
  "Quicklime (CaO) generates enough heat on contact with water to cause burns; adding caustic soda on top is reckless.": "",
  "this substance reacts violently with water, releasing a large amount of heat": "",
  "Quicklime (CaO) in water can reach 100 °C and cause severe burns. Always add the solid to the water slowly, never the reverse.": "",
  "sealed vessel over-pressurised and burst": "",
  "flying glass and a pressure wave — sealed systems on a heat source are how real labs get hurt; safe only because this lab is virtual": "",
  "on a real bench this one is never smelled directly — fume hood, waft only, and some not even then": "",
  "radioactive source: ionising radiation": "",
  "on a real bench this needs shielding, dosimetry and a licence; safe only because this lab is virtual": "",
